SQL Aggregates, GROUP BY & HAVING — Quick Revision Notes
Aggregates collapse many rows into one summary value. GROUP BY splits rows into buckets so the aggregate runs per bucket. HAVING filters groups (WHERE filters rows) — that's the whole trick.

🧠 Mind Map
                       AGGREGATE FUNCTIONS
                       (many rows → 1 value)
                                │
    ┌──────────┬──────────┬────┴─────┬──────────┬──────────┐
    │          │          │          │          │          │
  COUNT      SUM        AVG        MIN        MAX      (scalar funcs)
  how many   total      mean       smallest   largest    ISNULL, COALESCE
    │          │          │        (work on    (work on
    │          │          │         any type)   any type)
    │          │          │
    └──────────┴──────────┴──── GROUP BY bucket ────┐
                                                 │
                        ┌────────────────────────┴──────────────┐
                        │                                       │
                    WHERE (rows)                          HAVING (groups)
                  runs BEFORE grouping                 runs AFTER grouping
                  can't use aggregates                 CAN use aggregates


1️⃣ The 5 aggregate functions
SELECT MAX(years_employed) AS max_years_employed
FROM employees;

SELECT AVG(years_employed) FROM employees;   -- average
SELECT SUM(years_employed) FROM employees;   -- total
SELECT MIN(years_employed) FROM employees;   -- smallest
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employees;              -- how many rows
Function	Returns	Notes
COUNT(*)	number of rows	counts rows, even with NULLs
COUNT(col)	number of non-NULL values in col	skips NULLs ⚠️
SUM(col)	total	NULLs ignored
AVG(col)	mean	NULLs ignored
MIN(col) / MAX(col)	smallest / largest	works on numbers, text, dates
Alias the result with AS — otherwise the column header is ugly ((No column name)).


2️⃣ GROUP BY — one result row per group
-- Average years employed per role
SELECT role, AVG(years_employed)
FROM employees
GROUP BY role;

-- Total employee-years per building
SELECT building, SUM(years_employed)
FROM employees
GROUP BY building;

-- Number of employees per role
SELECT role, COUNT(role)
FROM employees
GROUP BY role;
🔑 Golden rule: every column in SELECT must be either inside an aggregate or listed in GROUP BY. Nothing else.

3️⃣ WHERE vs HAVING — filter rows vs filter groups
-- WHERE: filters ROWS before grouping (no aggregates allowed here)
SELECT role, COUNT(*) AS number_of_artists
FROM employees
WHERE role = 'Artist';        -- ✓ constant value, runs pre-grouping

-- HAVING: filters GROUPS after grouping (aggregates allowed here)
SELECT role, SUM(years_employed)
FROM employees
GROUP BY role
HAVING SUM(years_employed) > 10;   -- ✓ aggregate condition
Execution:  FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → TOP
                       ↑                        ↑
                 rows get filtered        groups get filtered
⚠️ WHERE score > 5 ✓ · WHERE SUM(score) > 5 ✗ (error — SUM doesn't exist yet) ⚠️ HAVING role = 'Artist' technically works, but WHERE role = 'Artist' is the right tool — it's faster.

4️⃣ Counting without GROUP BY (when you only need one group)
-- Number of Artists — no GROUP BY needed for a single value
SELECT role, COUNT(*) AS number_of_artists
FROM employees
WHERE role = 'Artist';

-- Total years employed by Engineers
SELECT role, SUM(years_employed)
FROM employees
WHERE role = 'Engineer'
GROUP BY role;               -- GROUP BY optional here, HAVING also possible
Three ways to filter one group — know them all:

Technique	Filter applied	Best when
WHERE role = 'Engineer'	before grouping	fastest, most readable ✓
GROUP BY role HAVING role = 'Engineer'	after grouping	works, but wasteful
WHERE + no GROUP BY, one output row	before grouping	only need a single total

